    "font" : "CMR10",
    "font size" : 10.909,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "where we use that tmfQ is the free E∞-Q-algebra on two generators c4 and c6 in degrees 8 and 12, respectively, that TMFQ ≃ tmfQ[∆−1] for ∆ = (c34 − c26)/1728, and that the containment above is a monomorphism onto those path components where the image of ∆ is invertible. Both the base-change of T and TQ to ( p B)Q are chosen such that the associated map from tmfQ sends c4 and c6 to their q-expansion maps. From the equivalences above, this shows that these oriented elliptic curves are equivalent over ( p B)Q. The fact that ( p B)Q has homotopy groups concentrated in even degrees also shows that the choice of equivalence is unique up to homotopy; even up to 3-homotopy. This yields the oriented elliptic curve T over B."
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 41,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 86.161, 124.692, 531.176, 203.282 ],
    "font" : "CMR10",
    "font size" : 10.909,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "To compute the formal group associated with T, notice that B is an E∞-KU-algebra, as it then follows that the formal group associated with T is Gm. Indeed, by [Lur18b, Pr.4.3.23], an orientation on T is an equivalence between its associated formal group and the Quillen formal group over B. As the Quillen formal group of a complex-periodic E∞-ring satisﬁes base-change, we obtain the desired result of the Quillen formal group Gm over KU. Alternatively, one can use the fact that the space of oriented formal groups over a complex-periodic"
